IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `coq-common`

-- Coroners Act 2003 (Queensland) -- part 3 divisions 4 and 5 as the family meets them: access to the investigation
-- documents (ss 52, 54, 55, 56, 57) and the physical evidence (ss 59, 60, 61, 62, 62A). The autopsy report, the police
-- report and the witness statements are often what a family needs to weigh a claim for compensation.
--
-- SEAT: a family member of the person who died, or another person with a sufficient interest; for physical evidence,
-- its owner (including the personal representative of the person who died). See NOTES.md.
--
-- Unofficial version. Based on content from the Queensland Legislation website at 6 November 2026. (c) The State of
-- Queensland, CC BY 4.0. See SOURCE-LICENSE.md.
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. See NOTES.md.

§ `Coroners Act 2003: documents and physical evidence`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 52, 54, 55, 56, 57 -- access to the investigation documents`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 52(1): "A coroner must not give a person access to an investigation document to the extent that the document-- (a)
-- is subject to legal professional privilege; or (b) contains information that is likely to-- (i) prevent a person from
-- receiving a fair trial; or (ii)-(vii) [prejudice an investigation, reveal a confidential source, endanger a person,
-- prejudice law enforcement methods or public safety, facilitate an escape]; or (c) contains information about a living
-- or dead person's personal affairs ... unless-- (i) the information is the result of a test ordered ... on application
-- under section 23A and the person wanting access is the applicant ...; or (ii) the information is relevant to a matter
-- mentioned in section 45(2) [who died, how, when, where, what caused the death] ...; or (d) contains information that
-- was obtained from a person under a requirement in another Act that compelled the person to give the information; or (e)
-- [rail safety and transport information]." s 54(2), (3): access "for purposes other than research purposes" with the
-- consent of the coroner, who "may consent only if-- (a) the coroner is satisfied the person has a sufficient interest
-- in the document; or (b) the coroner-- (i) considers the access is in the public interest; and (ii) to the extent
-- practicable, has consulted with and had regard to the views of a family member". Example: "an immediate member of the
-- deceased person's family". s 54(7): confidential information obtained under s 17 (or the Child Protection Act 1999 s
-- 159P, Public Health Act 2005 ss 56, 86) may be accessed only under those provisions. s 55: the coroner may impose
-- conditions; breach is an offence (100 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment). s 56(1): the coroner may refuse access
-- in the public interest even to an eligible person; (3) until a stated time; (4) the person may apply to amend or revoke
-- the refusal. s 57: where s 52 bars access to a document the coroner did not prepare (not a coronial document), the
-- coroner must pass the request to the entity that prepared it and tell the person in writing. Research access (s 53)
-- is out of scope.
DECLARE `What part of the document I want` IS ONE OF
    `information on who died, how, when, where, or what caused the death (the s 45(2) matters)`
    `the result of a test I applied for under s 23A`
    `other information about someone's personal affairs, such as health`
    `information not about anyone's personal affairs`

DECLARE `Kind of investigation document` IS ONE OF
    `a coronial document (prepared for the investigation, such as the autopsy report or the police report to the coroner)`
    `another investigation document (prepared by someone else, such as a police document or hospital records)`

DECLARE `What s 52 bars` IS ONE OF
    `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists`
    `legal professional privilege (s 52(1)(a))`
    `information likely to prejudice a fair trial, an investigation, a confidential source, someone's safety, law enforcement or an escape from custody (s 52(1)(b))`
    `information compelled from someone under another Act (s 52(1)(d))`
    `rail safety or transport information given to the coroner (s 52(1)(e))`

DECLARE `Can I see the investigation documents` IS ONE OF
    `yes, if the coroner consents: the coroner may consent, on conditions the coroner considers necessary (ss 54(2), (3), 55)`
    `yes: the coroner has consented; you must comply with any conditions, unless you have a reasonable excuse (maximum penalty 100 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment) (s 55)`
    `no: the coroner must not give you access to this part of the document (s 52(1))`
    `no: the coroner must not give you access to this part of the document (s 52(1)); as the coroner did not prepare it, the coroner must pass your request to the entity that prepared it and tell you in writing (s 57)`
    `no: the coroner may consent only if satisfied you have a sufficient interest, or if the access is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted as far as practicable (s 54(3))`
    `no: the coroner has refused access in the public interest (s 56(1)); you may apply to the coroner to amend or revoke the refusal (s 56(4))`
    `not under s 54: confidential information the coroner obtained under s 17 (or the Child Protection Act 1999 s 159P or the Public Health Act 2005 ss 56, 86) may be accessed only under those provisions (s 54(7))`

GIVEN `the document` IS A `Kind of investigation document`
      `the part I want` IS A `What part of the document I want`
      `the bar` IS A `What s 52 bars`
      `it is confidential information` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner refused in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner has consented` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I see the investigation documents`
`ss 52, 54-57 -- access to an investigation document` `the document` `the part I want` `the bar` `it is confidential information` `the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family` `the coroner refused in the public interest` `the coroner has consented` MEANS
    IF `it is confidential information`
    THEN `not under s 54: confidential information the coroner obtained under s 17 (or the Child Protection Act 1999 s 159P or the Public Health Act 2005 ss 56, 86) may be accessed only under those provisions (s 54(7))`
    ELSE IF `s 52 bars it`
         THEN CONSIDER `the document`
              WHEN `another investigation document (prepared by someone else, such as a police document or hospital records)` THEN `no: the coroner must not give you access to this part of the document (s 52(1)); as the coroner did not prepare it, the coroner must pass your request to the entity that prepared it and tell you in writing (s 57)`
              OTHERWISE `no: the coroner must not give you access to this part of the document (s 52(1))`
         ELSE IF NOT (`the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` OR `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family`)
              THEN `no: the coroner may consent only if satisfied you have a sufficient interest, or if the access is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted as far as practicable (s 54(3))`
              ELSE IF `the coroner refused in the public interest`
                   THEN `no: the coroner has refused access in the public interest (s 56(1)); you may apply to the coroner to amend or revoke the refusal (s 56(4))`
                   ELSE IF `the coroner has consented`
                        THEN `yes: the coroner has consented; you must comply with any conditions, unless you have a reasonable excuse (maximum penalty 100 penalty units or 2 years imprisonment) (s 55)`
                        ELSE `yes, if the coroner consents: the coroner may consent, on conditions the coroner considers necessary (ss 54(2), (3), 55)`
    WHERE
        `s 52 bars it` MEANS
            `a listed bar` OR `personal affairs not excepted`
        `a listed bar` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the bar`
            WHEN `nothing s 52(1)(a), (b), (d) or (e) lists` THEN FALSE
            OTHERWISE TRUE
        `personal affairs not excepted` MEANS
            CONSIDER `the part I want`
            WHEN `other information about someone's personal affairs, such as health` THEN TRUE
            OTHERWISE FALSE

@export Can I see the coroner's investigation documents, such as the autopsy report, police report or witness statements (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) ss 52, 54-57)?
GIVEN `the document` IS A `Kind of investigation document` @desc Whether the document was prepared for the coroner's investigation or by someone else
      `the part I want` IS A `What part of the document I want` @desc What information in the document you want
      `the bar` IS A `What s 52 bars` @desc Whether that part of the document is privileged, sensitive for law enforcement or safety, compelled under another Act, or rail or transport safety information
      `it is confidential information` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it confidential information the coroner obtained under section 17 of the Coroners Act, section 159P of the Child Protection Act 1999, or section 56 or 86 of the Public Health Act 2005?
      `the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the coroner satisfied you have a sufficient interest in the document (an immediate family member usually will)?
      `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the coroner consider your access is in the public interest, having consulted a family member as far as practicable?
      `the coroner refused in the public interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the coroner refused you access because disclosure would not be in the public interest?
      `the coroner has consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the coroner consented to your access?
GIVETH A `Can I see the investigation documents`
DECIDE `Can I see the investigation documents question` IS
    `ss 52, 54-57 -- access to an investigation document` `the document` `the part I want` `the bar` `it is confidential information` `the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family` `the coroner refused in the public interest` `the coroner has consented`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `ss 59-62 -- getting back property held as evidence`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 59(2): "However, this division does not apply to an investigation document, other than an investigation document
-- that was seized by a police officer for the investigation." s 60(1): "The coroner must order the physical evidence be
-- returned to its owner as soon as the coroner decides that the evidence is no longer required for-- (a) the
-- investigation; or (b) the investigation of another death under this Act; or (c) a proceeding for an offence relating
-- to the death." (2): "However, the coroner must not order the return ... if-- (a) it is not lawful for the owner to
-- possess the physical evidence; or (b) under the guidelines issued by the State Coroner, it is not desirable that the
-- physical evidence be returned to its owner because of its nature, condition and value." (3): "owner means-- (a) the
-- person who appears to the coroner to be the lawful owner ...; or (b) for something that was owned by a person who has
-- died--the deceased person's personal representative." s 61(1): it becomes State property if the coroner cannot return
-- it after reasonable efforts, or does not order its return for a s 60(2) reason. s 62: "Until the physical evidence is
-- returned or becomes State property, the coroner must allow its owner to inspect it and, if it is a document, to copy
-- it, unless it is impracticable or would be unreasonable".
DECLARE `Will my property be returned` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner must order it returned to you as soon as the coroner decides it is no longer required (s 60(1))`
    `not yet: it is still required; until it is returned the coroner must let you inspect it, and copy it if it is a document, unless that is impracticable or unreasonable (s 62)`
    `no: it is not lawful for you to possess it, so it must not be returned and becomes State property (ss 60(2)(a), 61(1)(b))`
    `no: under the State Coroner's guidelines it is not desirable to return it because of its nature, condition and value, so it becomes State property (ss 60(2)(b), 61(1)(b))`
    `no: the coroner could not return it to you after reasonable efforts, so it has become State property (s 61(1)(a))`
    `s 60 does not apply: you are not its owner (the person who appears to the coroner to be its lawful owner, or the personal representative of the person who died)`
    `division 5 does not apply: an investigation document not seized by a police officer is dealt with under division 4 (s 59(2))`

GIVEN `it is an investigation document not seized by police` IS A BOOLEAN
      `I am the owner` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner decided it is no longer required` IS A BOOLEAN
      `it is lawful for me to possess it` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the guidelines make return undesirable` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner could not return it after reasonable efforts` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Will my property be returned`
`ss 59-62 -- return of physical evidence` `it is an investigation document not seized by police` `I am the owner` `the coroner decided it is no longer required` `it is lawful for me to possess it` `the guidelines make return undesirable` `the coroner could not return it after reasonable efforts` MEANS
    IF `it is an investigation document not seized by police`
    THEN `division 5 does not apply: an investigation document not seized by a police officer is dealt with under division 4 (s 59(2))`
    ELSE IF NOT `I am the owner`
         THEN `s 60 does not apply: you are not its owner (the person who appears to the coroner to be its lawful owner, or the personal representative of the person who died)`
         ELSE IF NOT `it is lawful for me to possess it`
              THEN `no: it is not lawful for you to possess it, so it must not be returned and becomes State property (ss 60(2)(a), 61(1)(b))`
              ELSE IF `the guidelines make return undesirable`
                   THEN `no: under the State Coroner's guidelines it is not desirable to return it because of its nature, condition and value, so it becomes State property (ss 60(2)(b), 61(1)(b))`
                   ELSE IF NOT `the coroner decided it is no longer required`
                        THEN `not yet: it is still required; until it is returned the coroner must let you inspect it, and copy it if it is a document, unless that is impracticable or unreasonable (s 62)`
                        ELSE IF `the coroner could not return it after reasonable efforts`
                             THEN `no: the coroner could not return it to you after reasonable efforts, so it has become State property (s 61(1)(a))`
                             ELSE `yes: the coroner must order it returned to you as soon as the coroner decides it is no longer required (s 60(1))`

@export The police or coroner took property (for example the belongings of the person who died) as evidence. Will it be returned to me (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) ss 59-62)?
GIVEN `it is an investigation document not seized by police` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it a document connected to the investigation that a police officer did not seize (for example a report given to the coroner)?
      `I am the owner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you its owner: the person who appears to the coroner to be its lawful owner, or, for something the person who died owned, their personal representative (executor or administrator)?
      `the coroner decided it is no longer required` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the coroner decided it is no longer required for the investigation, another death investigation, or a prosecution for an offence relating to the death?
      `it is lawful for me to possess it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is it lawful for you to possess it?
      `the guidelines make return undesirable` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Under the State Coroner's guidelines, is it not desirable to return it because of its nature, condition and value (for example a cracked safety helmet)?
      `the coroner could not return it after reasonable efforts` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the coroner been unable to return it to you despite reasonable efforts (for example you could not be found)?
GIVETH A `Will my property be returned`
DECIDE `Will my property be returned question` IS
    `ss 59-62 -- return of physical evidence` `it is an investigation document not seized by police` `I am the owner` `the coroner decided it is no longer required` `it is lawful for me to possess it` `the guidelines make return undesirable` `the coroner could not return it after reasonable efforts`

-- ===================================================================================================================
§§ `s 62A -- access to an inquest exhibit`
-- ===================================================================================================================

-- s 62A(2): "The person may access the physical evidence exhibit only if-- (a) the coroner has given consent for the
-- access; or (b) [police access for an offence]." (3): "The coroner may consent to a person having access to a physical
-- evidence exhibit only if-- (a) the coroner is satisfied the person has a sufficient interest in the physical evidence
-- exhibit; or (b) the coroner-- (i) considers the access is in the public interest; and (ii) to the extent practicable,
-- has consulted with and had regard to the views of a family member". Example: a person at an inquest wanting to test
-- the exhibit. (1): the section is for a person other than the owner (the owner has s 62).
DECLARE `Can I access the inquest exhibit` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the coroner has consented to your access (s 62A(2)(a))`
    `possibly: the coroner may consent to your access (s 62A(3))`
    `no: you may access the exhibit only with the coroner's consent, and the coroner may consent only if satisfied you have a sufficient interest, or if the access is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted (s 62A(2), (3))`
    `s 62A does not apply to the owner: the owner may inspect it under s 62`

GIVEN `I am the owner` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family` IS A BOOLEAN
      `the coroner has consented` IS A BOOLEAN
GIVETH A `Can I access the inquest exhibit`
`s 62A -- access to an inquest exhibit` `I am the owner` `the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family` `the coroner has consented` MEANS
    IF `I am the owner`
    THEN `s 62A does not apply to the owner: the owner may inspect it under s 62`
    ELSE IF `the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` OR `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family`
         THEN IF `the coroner has consented`
              THEN `yes: the coroner has consented to your access (s 62A(2)(a))`
              ELSE `possibly: the coroner may consent to your access (s 62A(3))`
         ELSE `no: you may access the exhibit only with the coroner's consent, and the coroner may consent only if satisfied you have a sufficient interest, or if the access is in the public interest and a family member has been consulted (s 62A(2), (3))`

@export Can I see or test an exhibit tendered at the inquest, for example to have it examined for my claim (Coroners Act 2003 (Qld) s 62A)?
GIVEN `I am the owner` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you the owner of the exhibit?
      `the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the coroner satisfied you have a sufficient interest in the exhibit?
      `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the coroner consider your access in the public interest, having consulted a family member as far as practicable?
      `the coroner has consented` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the coroner consented to your access?
GIVETH A `Can I access the inquest exhibit`
DECIDE `Can I access the inquest exhibit question` IS
    `s 62A -- access to an inquest exhibit` `I am the owner` `the coroner is satisfied I have a sufficient interest` `the coroner considers access in the public interest and consulted the family` `the coroner has consented`
